drm/esp32s31-lcd: fullscreen present without an atomic commit (PRESENT_MODE_FB),
no hopeless engine probes, and present_stats readable without debugfs

This patch is cumulative on 0058 (esp32s31-lcd.c) and 0049 (esp32s31_drm.h).
Copy the files as follows:
  esp32s31-lcd.c   -> linux-71-port/drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/
  esp32s31_drm.h   -> linux-71-port/include/uapi/drm/
It first shipped in kernel #393 (2026-09-26). The lvdesk side is in
lvdesk/kms.c, `kms_fs_dirty()`.

THE QUESTION. The owner asked about fullscreen frame-rate dips in
glxgears. The fullscreen present (a VidMode or render-scale client) is a
DIRTYFB on the mode framebuffer. lvdesk's stage timer (LVDESK_FSGSTAGE=1)
put that call at 3.0 ms on average and 28.9 ms at worst.

WHAT DIRTYFB SPENT. This comes from the new present_stats timers
(scripts/board/fs-present-probe.sh, 20 s of fullscreen glxgears at render
scale with 2 SHM buffers, kernel #392):
- The ioctl took 2.2 ms per call as lvdesk sees it.
- pipe_update() was 0.55-0.62 ms of that: copy_one 0.35-0.39 ms (PPA submit
  plus drain of the previous job) and the flush 0.08 ms.
- The remaining ~1.6 ms is the atomic commit around it: state duplication,
  check, commit tail and cleanup, which is DRM core code executing from XIP
  flash.
- The worst case, 28-39 ms, was INSIDE copy_one (ppa max = upd max). It came
  2 or 3 times per 20-30 s, which is exactly one every 512 presents at about
  50/s. The adaptive engine picker probes the non-preferred engine one op in
  512 even when that engine is far behind, and a scaled 400x240->800x480
  frame on the CPU costs 17-37 ms (ppa_table bucket 7: cpu 29 ms against
  the PPA's 0.35).

THE FIX, three parts:
1. `DRM_IOCTL_ESP32S31_PRESENT` takes a flags word. `PRESENT_MODE_FB` runs
   DIRTYFB's damage path for one rectangle: lattice snap, source flush,
   CPU/PPA copy, cursor repaint and the recorder notify. It holds all the
   modeset locks the commit would hold, and it does no atomic commit.
   - The struct grew from 16 to 20 bytes. drm_ioctl() reconciles the sizes
     in both directions, so an old lvdesk (flags=0) gets the old ioctl.
   - A new lvdesk on an old kernel is refused with EINVAL and falls back to
     DIRTYFB.
   - lvdesk: `LVDESK_NOFSPRESENT=1` forces DIRTYFB.
2. `ppa_explore_max` (default 8): never probe the other engine while it
   costs more than 8x the chosen one.
   - A probe cannot flip a pick that is 50x ahead, because the switch needs
     the other engine 15% cheaper.
   - A degrading engine closes the ratio through its own EMA, which is
     updated on every op it runs, so the probes then resume.
   - The latency guard is unchanged. 0 restores the old behaviour.
3. `present_stats`: upd, flush, ppa and fsp timers. Read the parameter for
   the numbers and write anything to reset them. It is readable on the
   shipping DIAG=0 kernel, which has no debugfs.

RESULT on one boot, #393, fs-present-probe with 30 s windows of fullscreen
glxgears:

                        old (DIRTYFB, explore=0)   new (PRESENT_MODE_FB, explore=8)
  present ioctl < 1 ms      2 of 1,496               1,246 of 1,608
  present ioctl >= 12 ms    3                        0
  worst present ioctl       38.0 ms                  2.8 ms  (fsp max 2.07)
  presents in 30 s          1,495 (49.3/s)           1,607 (52.9/s)
  gaps 50-100 ms            12                       10

The fresh-boot arms and the GLQuake numbers are in
artifacts/gl/glquake/README.md.

WHAT IT DOES NOT FIX. The visible dips are 50-100 ms present-to-present
gaps, about 10 per 30 s. They did NOT line up with the present:
- lvdesk now logs the preceding present's ioctl time with every long frame
  (`/dN` in the SIGUSR1 report). The dip clusters carried d1-d6, and the
  30 ms spikes produced lone 41 ms frames.
- The dips were unchanged by the fix (12 against 10 per 30 s).
- They were unchanged by pinning the client to CPU0 or CPU1 (13, 13 and 12
  per 20 s), and they came with no major faults.
They are on the client or scheduling side, and they are still open.
